## 17. Context: How the Model Sees Your Codebase

**Context** is everything the model can "see" when responding to you.
It's the most important concept for getting good results.

### What Goes Into Context

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  SYSTEM INSTRUCTIONS                      │
│  • System prompt (OpenCode's core rules)  │
│  • Agent instructions                     │
│  • Loaded skills                          │
│  • Config file settings                   │
├─────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  CONVERSATION HISTORY                     │
│  • Your messages                          │
│  • Model's responses                      │
│  • Tool call results                      │
├─────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  REFERENCES                               │
│  • Files the model has read               │
│  • Grep/search results                    │
│  • Web fetch results                      │
├─────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  TOOL SCHEMAS                             │
│  • Available tools and their arguments    │
│  • Custom tool definitions                │
└─────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

### The Context Window

Every model has a **context window** — a maximum number of tokens it can
process at once.

| Model | Context Window |
|---|---|
| DeepSeek V4.1 Flash Free | ~1,000,000 tokens |
| Claude Sonnet 5.5 | ~200,000 tokens |
| GPT-5 | ~128,000 tokens |

When the context window fills up, the oldest messages get "forgotten"
(removed from context).

### Managing Context

**The sidebar shows your context usage:**

```
Context
147,529 tokens
37% used
$5.56 spent
```

When usage gets high (80%+), you can:

1. **Start a fresh session** — `/new` clears the slate
2. **Compact the session** — OpenCode can summarize old messages
3. **Be more specific** — instead of "fix the bug", say "fix the bug in
   `src/auth/login.ts` on line 42"

### References: Persistent Context

**References** are files or directories you want in context automatically:

```jsonc
{
  "references": [
    { "type": "local", "path": "docs/architecture.md" },
    { "type": "local", "path": "CONTRIBUTING.md" },
  ],
}
```

Now every prompt includes the architecture doc and contributing guide
without you having to ask.

### Git References

You can also reference files from a remote repository:

```jsonc
{
  "references": [
    {
      "type": "git",
      "repository": "https://github.com/acme/standards",
      "branch": "main",
    },
  ],
}
```

### Practical Example: Use References

1. Add to `opencode.jsonc`:

```jsonc
{
  "references": [
    { "type": "local", "path": "docs/spec.md" },
    { "type": "local", "path": "README.md" },
  ],
}
```

2. Ask: *"What language features does Sloperator support?"*
3. The model already has `docs/spec.md` in context — it answers immediately
   without needing to read any files.

### Instruction Entries: Keyed Context

For dynamic context that changes during a session (like the current
deployment target or branch), use **instruction entries**:

```
/instruction set deploy-target production
```

This injects `<context key="deploy-target">production</context>` into the
model's instructions. The model sees it on every turn without you repeating it.

