## 19. Tool Permissions

You control which tools the agent can use automatically and which require
your approval.

### Permission Rules

In `opencode.jsonc`:

```jsonc
{
  "permissions": [
    { "action": "shell", "resource": "*", "effect": "ask" },
    { "action": "edit", "resource": "*", "effect": "allow" },
    { "action": "webfetch", "resource": "*", "effect": "deny" },
  ],
}
```

Each rule has three parts:

| Part | What it is | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `action` | Which tool or action | `shell`, `edit`, `read`, `webfetch` |
| `resource` | What it applies to | `*` (everything), `git push *` |
| `effect` | What happens | `allow`, `ask`, `deny` |

### Evaluation Order

Rules are evaluated **last match wins**:

1. Put broad defaults first
2. Put narrow exceptions later

```jsonc
{
  "permissions": [
    { "action": "shell", "resource": "*", "effect": "ask" },      // Broad: ask for all shell
    { "action": "shell", "resource": "bootdev run *", "effect": "allow" },  // Exception: allow bootdev
  ],
}
```

### Practical Example: Configure Permissions

1. Update your `opencode.jsonc`:

```jsonc
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "model": "opencode/deepseek-v4.1-flash-free",
  "permissions": [
    { "action": "run_slop", "resource": "*", "effect": "allow" },
    { "action": "shell", "resource": "*", "effect": "ask" },
    { "action": "shell", "resource": "bootdev run *", "effect": "allow" },
  ],
}
```

2. Ask the agent to run `pytest -q`
3. You'll see a permission prompt — approve it once
4. Ask the agent to run `examples/hello.slop` with `run_slop`
5. It runs without asking (the `run_slop` rule allows it)

### Default Permissions

| Action | Default |
|---|---|
| Most tools | `allow` |
| `read` on `.env` files | `ask` |
| `external_directory` | `allow` |

